course: everyday
lesson: 416
title: «Free V-bucks» and other scams
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Kai found a video called «FREE V-BUCKS 2026 WORKING!!!» with a man shouting and coins exploding on the screen. Kai was excited, sure he had found a secret. «Just enter your Fortnite login and the V-bucks drop in your account,» said the man. Kai was halfway through typing his password when his dad walked past, read the screen over his shoulder and said: «Ah. The famous free money that needs your password.» He explained that the site was built to steal accounts. They reported the video together, and Kai changed his password just in case. Kai felt silly for a minute, then rather proud that he now knew the trick.
explain: Not everyone was so lucky. Priya's friend Amber typed her login into a «free skins» website. The next morning her account was gone: two years of skins, levels and saved builds, sold to a stranger. Priya told the whole class at news time. Theo then confessed that he had clicked a «free gems» link and his family's computer went slow and weird, popping up adverts for things nobody wanted. His dad spent a whole Saturday cleaning it and was grumpy until Sunday.
explain: Some tricks were almost funny. A player in Roblox chat wrote to Ruby: «I'll give you 1000 free Robux if you send me yours first.» Ruby laughed out loud: «If you have so many, why do you need mine?» Leo got a message saying he had «WON A FREE CONSOLE!!!» from a competition he had never entered; it wanted his address and his mum's card details «for delivery». Jayden tried a video's «glitch for free coins» four times; the only thing it gave him was a crashed game.
explain: Then Mia got a message from her friend Ella: «quick can u send me ur gift card code, I'll pay u back tmrw». It was Ella's name and Ella's picture, but it did not sound like Ella, who always uses ten emojis. Mia asked her at school. Ella's account had been hacked, and the hacker was asking everyone on her list. That evening Noah and his dad made a scam checklist and stuck it on the fridge: it asks for a password or a code; it is too good to be true; it rushes you; it wants you to keep it secret. Zara used the same checklist when a man rang her grandma «from the bank» and asked for her card number, and Grandma put the phone down and rang her bank herself, on the real number printed on the back of her card.
fact: No real game company ever asks for your password to give you free coins, skins or prizes.
fact: Websites and videos that promise «free V-bucks» or «free Robux» are scams made to steal accounts or get views.
fact: A stolen account can lose years of progress, and it is often sold to strangers.
fact: Links that promise free things can also bring harmful programs that make a computer slow or unsafe.
fact: You cannot win a competition you never entered.
fact: A stranger who asks you to give first and promises to give more later is almost always tricking you.
fact: Codes and currency sold cheaply by other kids or strangers are often stolen or fake; only official shops are safe.
fact: A message can carry a friend's name and picture and still come from a hacker who took the account.
fact: Common signs of a scam are: it asks for passwords or codes, it is too good to be true, it rushes you, and it wants secrecy.
fact: Scammers use the same tricks in games, on the phone and by email, so the same checklist works everywhere.
fact: If you are tricked, telling a grown-up quickly, contacting the game's support team and changing your password limits the damage.
exercise: What did the «free V-bucks» video really want from Kai? | His login, so it could steal his account | His help to test a brand new game before everyone else | A review of the video for the channel | His address to post him a prize
exercise: How did Kai feel when he first found the video? | Excited that he had found a secret | Scared that someone was watching him | Angry with his dad for interrupting | Bored because he already had V-bucks
exercise: What did Kai and his dad do after spotting the trick? | Reported the video and changed his password | Sent the video to all his friends | Typed in the password just to see what happened | Left a comment thanking the man
exercise: What happened to Amber after she used the «free skins» website? | She lost her account and two years of progress | She got all the skins she wanted, for free, for ever | Her game ran a bit faster than before | Her account was moved to a new server
exercise: What did Priya do when she heard about Amber's account? | Warned her whole class about the trick | Tried the same website to check it | Kept the story secret to protect Amber | Asked Amber for the website's address
exercise: Why did Theo's computer go slow after he clicked «free gems»? | The link brought a harmful program with it | The gems were far too big for the old computer | The game was downloading new levels | His dad had switched on a slow mode
exercise: Why did Ruby laugh at the player offering 1000 free Robux? | Someone with so many would not need hers first | The player had spelt «Robux» wrongly | She had already got loads and loads of Robux of her own | The player was her cousin joking
exercise: Why could Leo not have really won the free console? | He had never entered any competition | Consoles are too big to post to houses | Only grown-ups can win a console | The message came on a weekday
exercise: What did the «win a console» message ask Leo for? | His address and his mum's card details | Only his favourite colour for the box it came in | A photo of his old console to swap | His teacher's name and school address
exercise: What did Jayden's «glitch for free coins» really give him? | A crashed game and no coins | A hundred coins and a new skin | A ban from the game for ever | A secret level nobody had seen
exercise: Why was the gift-card message from «Ella» not really from Ella? | Her account had been hacked by someone else | Ella was testing whether Mia was kind | Ella's little brother had borrowed her tablet for the day | The game sent it to everyone by mistake
exercise: What made Mia suspicious of the message? | It did not sound like Ella and wanted a code | It came in the middle of a school day, during maths | It had too many emojis in it for Ella | It was written in a different language
exercise: What was wise about how Mia checked? | She asked Ella face to face at school | She replied to the message to ask if it was really her | She sent half the code just in case | She asked the message for a password
exercise: Which is on Noah's scam checklist? | It rushes you to act right now | It comes from a game you play every day | It has pictures and bright colours | It arrives in the evening after school
exercise: How did Zara help her grandma? | She spotted the bank call as a scam | She gave the caller Grandma's card | She called the bank number from the call | She told Grandma to wait for a letter
exercise: Why does Noah's checklist work for phone calls as well as games? | Scammers use the same tricks everywhere | Phones and games are made by one company | Grandmas also play online games | Phone scammers only call children
exercise: Which kind of courage did Kai show after nearly being tricked? | Admitting he nearly fell for it | Keeping it secret from his dad for ever | Trying the site once more to check | Blaming the man in the video for lying
test: «bro free vbucks site, legit, just put ur login in, my cousin did it». What's the smart answer? | No, anything that wants my password is a scam | Fine, if your cousin did it it's legit | Only if it gives at least 5000 | I'll use my mum's login, not mine
test: Ollie's mate: «cheap vbucks, 5 quid for a code, half the shop price». Most likely? | The code is fake or stolen and won't work | It's a real bargain, buy two | His mate works for the game company and gets codes | Codes are always cheaper from friends
test: «you WON a free PS6!!! send address + card for delivery». Leo never entered anything. True or false: he might really have won? | False, you can't win what you never entered | True, some prizes are picked at random from everyone | True, if the message has a logo | False, consoles are never given away
test: Robux guy in chat: «give me yours first, I'll double it, promise». What's going on? | It's a «you first» trick, he'll keep them | He's rich and just feels generous | It's a game event run by Roblox | He needs them to unlock his doubler
test: Theo, red-faced: «I only clicked it once, it said free gems». Teacher: «And then?» What most likely happened next? | The computer got slow and weird | The gems arrived the next morning | Nothing at all, one click is safe | The game sent him a thank-you
test: Text from «Ella»: «urgent!! need ur gift card code, don't tell anyone». Which two signs of a scam does it show? | It rushes you and wants secrecy | It uses the word «urgent» and a name | It is short and has no emojis at all | It came by text and not by email
test: Jayden: «this vid says do the glitch for free coins, 2 million views so it must work». What's true? | Views don't make it true; it's made for views | Two million views prove it really works for everyone | Only videos with fewer views are fake | It works if you try it ten times
test: Amber sobbing: «my acc's gone, all my skins, 2 years!!» What should she do first? | Tell a grown-up and contact the game's support | Make a new account and say nothing | Try another free skins site to get them back | Ask the hacker nicely in chat to return it
test: Grandma: «nice man from the bank wants my card number to keep it safe». Zara's best answer? | Hang up and ring the bank's real number | Read it out slowly so he gets every number right | Give him only the first half of it | Ask him to say please and then give it
test: Kai: «the YouTuber had coins exploding everywhere, he looked well rich». What should Kai remember? | Flashy videos can be made just to trick you | Rich YouTubers never tell lies to fans | Exploding coins mean the site is real | He should comment to ask for some
test: «pls dont tell ur parents about this offer, its secret». Why is the secrecy a warning? | Scammers don't want a grown-up to spot it | Secret offers are always the best ones | Parents would want the offer for themselves | Secrets make a game more exciting
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a website promising complimentary in-game currency requests the user's password. | To obtain unauthorised control of the user's account | To verify the user's age in accordance with the law | To deposit the promised currency more rapidly | To register the user for a forthcoming competition
exam: It is given that a pupil receives notification of a prize in respect of a competition he did not enter. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The notification is fraudulent, as no entry was made | The prize should be claimed without delay | The pupil's address may safely be supplied | The prize is genuine if a logo is displayed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a message, bearing a friend's name, which solicits a gift card code? | It may originate from a person who has compromised the friend's account | It is necessarily genuine, the name being correct | It may be answered in part without risk | It constitutes an official communication from the game
exam: Determine which of the following does NOT constitute a recognised indication of fraud. | That the offer is made through an official shop | That the offer appears too favourable to be true | That the recipient is pressed to act at once | That the recipient is requested to maintain secrecy
